Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>9  A finished application</H1>
<HR>
Contacts now does everything an address book must. This chapter adds what makes an application pleasant to
use and a good citizen of the desktop: undo, choices that are remembered, interactive help, information
about a file, and taking care of your work when the desktop shuts down. At the end is the finished
<CODE>!Contacts</CODE>, and a checklist of what a well-behaved RISC OS application does.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Contacts" WIDTH=989 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Undo</H2>
Everybody deletes the wrong thing sometimes. An application that can undo it makes people confident enough
to try things. The Style Guide gives undo and redo the keys F8 and F9.<P>
The idea is to describe each change as an object that knows how to make the change and how to take it back:
a <I>command</I>. An object with <CODE>do</CODE> and <CODE>undo</CODE> methods, and a name to show in the
menu:<P>
<PRE>
{ name: 'Delete',
  do: () =&gt; book.remove(bob.id),
  undo: () =&gt; book.put(bob) }
</PRE>
Instead of changing the book directly, the program gives the command to an <CODE>Undo</CODE> object, which
carries it out and remembers it. The last toolkit module for Part One keeps two stacks - the changes that can
be undone, and the ones that have been undone and could be redone:<P>
<PRE>
// Undo - remembers what has been done so it can be undone and
// redone. Each change is a command object:
//
//   undo.do({ name: 'Delete', do() { ... }, undo() { ... } })
//   undo.undo(); undo.redo();
//   undo.canUndo, undo.canRedo, undo.undoName   ('Delete' ...)
//   undo.clear();  undo.on('change', ...)       (for shading menus)
import { Emitter } from './Emitter';

export class Undo extends Emitter {
  constructor(limit = 50) {
    super();
    this.limit = limit;              // how many changes to remember
    this.done = [];                  // changes that can be undone
    this.undone = [];                // changes that can be redone
  }

  /** Make a change (cmd.do()) and remember it. */
  do(cmd) {
    cmd.do();
    this.done.push(cmd);
    if (this.done.length &gt; this.limit) this.done.shift();
    this.undone = [];                // a new change: no redoing
    this.emit('change');
  }

  undo() {
    const cmd = this.done.pop();
    if (!cmd) return false;
    cmd.undo();
    this.undone.push(cmd);
    this.emit('change');
    return true;
  }

  redo() {
    const cmd = this.undone.pop();
    if (!cmd) return false;
    cmd.do();
    this.done.push(cmd);
    this.emit('change');
    return true;
  }

  clear() {
    this.done = [];
    this.undone = [];
    this.emit('change');
  }

  get canUndo() { return this.done.length &gt; 0; }
  get canRedo() { return this.undone.length &gt; 0; }
  get undoName() { return this.done.at(-1)?.name ?? ''; }
  get redoName() { return this.undone.at(-1)?.name ?? ''; }
}
</PRE>
<CODE>do</CODE> carries out a command and pushes it onto <CODE>done</CODE>. <CODE>undo</CODE> pops the last
one, takes it back, and pushes it onto <CODE>undone</CODE>; <CODE>redo</CODE> does the reverse. Making a new
change empties <CODE>undone</CODE>: once you've done something new, redoing the old change would make no
sense. Only the last 50 changes are kept, so memory isn't filled up. <CODE>at(-1)</CODE> is the last item
(chapter 3), and each change of state emits <CODE>'change'</CODE>, so the menus and toolbar can follow.<P>
Each document has its own <CODE>Undo</CODE> (<CODE>this.undo = new Undo()</CODE> in the
<CODE>Document</CODE> constructor), so undoing in one address book never touches another.<P>

<H3>Changes as commands</H3>
The three changes in <CODE>MainWindow</CODE> become commands:<P>
<PRE>
  async add() {
    const v = await editCard(this.app.task, {}, 'New contact');
    this.focus();
    if (!v) return;
    const book = this.doc.book;
    let c = null;
    this.doc.undo.do({
      name: 'New',
      do: () =&gt; { c = book.add({ ...v, id: c?.id }); },
      undo: () =&gt; book.remove(c.id),
    });
    this.list.select(c);
    this.list.ensureVisible(c);
  }
  async edit(c = this.list.selected()[0]) {
    if (!c) return;
    const v = await editCard(this.app.task, c, nameOf(c));
    this.focus();
    if (!v) return;
    const book = this.doc.book, before = book.get(c.id);
    this.doc.undo.do({
      name: 'Change',
      do: () =&gt; book.put({ ...before, ...v }),
      undo: () =&gt; book.put(before),
    });
  }
  async remove() {
    const gone = this.list.selected();
    if (!gone.length) return;
    if (this.app.prefs.confirm) {
      const what = gone.length === 1 ? nameOf(gone[0])
        : `these ${gone.length} contacts`;
      const r = await query({ task: this.app.task, title: 'Contacts',
        message: `Delete ${what}?`, buttons: ['Delete', 'Cancel'] });
      this.focus();
      if (r !== 'Delete') return;
    }
    const book = this.doc.book;
    this.doc.undo.do({
      name: 'Delete',
      do: () =&gt; { for (const c of gone) book.remove(c.id); },
      undo: () =&gt; { for (const c of gone) book.put(c); },
    });
  }
  undo() { this.doc.undo.undo(); }
  redo() { this.doc.undo.redo(); }
</PRE>
Undoing needs the book to put back a contact exactly as it was, with the same <CODE>id</CODE>. The
<CODE>Book</CODE> gets one more method for that:<P>
<PRE>
  put(c) {
    this.contacts.set(c.id, c);
    this.nextId = Math.max(this.nextId, c.id + 1);
    this.emit('change', { updated: c });
  }
</PRE>
Look at the <I>New</I> command. The first time it runs, <CODE>c</CODE> is <CODE>null</CODE>, so
<CODE>c?.id</CODE> is <CODE>undefined</CODE> and the book gives the contact a new id. If it is undone and
then redone, <CODE>c</CODE> is the contact it made before, so the redone contact gets the same id again -
important, because a later command may refer to it by that id.<P>
The <I>Change</I> command keeps a copy of the contact as it was (<CODE>before</CODE>), and <I>Delete</I>
keeps the contacts it removes (<CODE>gone</CODE>). Importing a file from chapter 8 is one command too, so a
whole import can be undone in one go.<P>

<H3>Showing what can be undone</H3>
The Undo and Redo buttons in the toolbar are shaded when there's nothing to undo or redo, and the menu says
what would be undone:<P>
<PRE>
  shade() {
    const n = this.list.selected().length;
    this.bar.shade('edit', n !== 1);
    this.bar.shade('delete', n === 0);
    this.bar.shade('undo', !this.doc.undo.canUndo);
    this.bar.shade('redo', !this.doc.undo.canRedo);
  }
</PRE>
<PRE>
        { text: () =&gt; `Undo ${u.undoName}`.trim(), key: 'F8',
          shaded: () =&gt; !u.canUndo, action: () =&gt; this.undo() },
        { text: () =&gt; `Redo ${u.redoName}`.trim(), key: 'F9',
          shaded: () =&gt; !u.canRedo, action: () =&gt; this.redo() },
</PRE>
A menu item's <CODE>text</CODE> can be a function, called each time the menu opens - so it reads
<I>Undo Delete</I> after a delete. <CODE>trim()</CODE> takes the space off the end when there's no name.<P>
