<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Choices</H2>
Most applications let you change how they behave, and remember your choices. The Style Guide says how:<P>
<UL>
<LI>A <I>Choices...</I> item on the icon bar menu opens a dialogue box.<P>
<LI>The box has four buttons: <I>Default</I> puts the standard settings in the box, <I>Cancel</I> closes
it, <I>Set</I> uses the settings now, and <I>Save</I> uses them and keeps them for next time.<P>
<LI>Choices are saved in the Choices directory, not beside the application: the application may be on a
disc you can't write to, or shared by several people.<P>
</UL>
The desktop provides <CODE>choices.read(name, defaults)</CODE> and <CODE>choices.write(name, value)</CODE>,
which keep a JSON file called <CODE>name</CODE> in the Choices directory (<CODE>Choices:Contacts</CODE>).
<CODE>read</CODE> returns the defaults for anything not saved yet. Contacts' module <CODE>Choices</CODE>
uses them:<P>
<PRE>
// Choices - the settings a user can change, kept in Choices:Contacts
// so they stay the same next time (the RISC OS Style Guide's way).
//
//   const prefs = await loadChoices();     { sort, confirm }
//   openChoices(task, prefs, changed)    the Choices... dialogue box
import { choices } from 'riscos';
import { Form } from './Toolkit/Form';
import { SORTS } from './Model';

export const DEFAULTS = { sort: 'last', confirm: true };

export const loadChoices = () =&gt; choices.read('Contacts', DEFAULTS);

let form = null;

/** Open the Choices box; changed(prefs) when they're Set or Saved. */
export async function openChoices(task, prefs, changed) {
  const names = Object.values(SORTS);
  form ??= new Form(task, {
    title: 'Contacts choices',
    help: 'Choose how Contacts behaves.',
    fields: [
      { name: 'sort', label: 'Sort by', width: 160, choices: names,
        help: 'The order contacts are listed in.' },
      { name: 'confirm', label: 'Ask before deleting', option: true,
        help: 'Ask before deleting contacts.' },
    ],
    buttons: ['Default', 'Cancel', 'Set', 'Save'],
    stay: ['Default'],
  });
  // Default: put the default settings in the box (not used yet)
  form.off?.();
  form.off = form.on('button', () =&gt; form.set(shown(DEFAULTS)));
  const r = await form.run(shown(prefs));
  if (!r) return;
  const sort = Object.keys(SORTS)
    .find((k) =&gt; SORTS[k] === r.values.sort);
  Object.assign(prefs, { sort, confirm: r.values.confirm });
  if (r.button === 'Save') choices.write('Contacts', prefs);
  changed(prefs);
}

// the box shows the sort order's name
const shown = (p) =&gt; ({ ...p, sort: SORTS[p.sort] });
</PRE>
The box is a <CODE>Form</CODE> with a pop-up menu for the sort order and an option icon for confirming
deletions. <I>Default</I> is named in <CODE>stay</CODE>: pressing it doesn't close the box, but emits
<CODE>'button'</CODE>, and the program puts the default settings in the fields. The box shows the sort
order's name (<I>Surname</I>), while the program stores its key (<CODE>last</CODE>); <CODE>shown</CODE>
turns one into the other.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/ContactsChoices" WIDTH=409 HEIGHT=173></CENTER>
<CODE>!RunImage</CODE> reads the choices when Contacts starts (<CODE>prefs: await loadChoices()</CODE>)
and puts <I>Choices...</I> on the icon bar menu:<P>
<PRE>
  const menu = () =&gt; new Menu('Contacts', [
    { text: 'Info', submenu: info },
    { text: 'Choices...', help: 'Change how Contacts behaves.',
      action: () =&gt; openChoices(task, app.prefs,
        () =&gt; app.refreshAll()) },
    { text: 'Quit', action: quit, help: 'Stop Contacts.' },
  ]);
</PRE>
When the choices are set, every open book is listed again in the new order. The confirm choice is used in
<CODE>remove</CODE>, above: with it off, Delete deletes straight away - which is safe, now that it can be
undone.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Interactive help</H2>
RISC OS's <I>!Help</I> application (in the Apps icon's directory) shows a line or two about whatever is
under the pointer. For it to work in your application, every window, icon and menu item should have help
text. It is easy to add, and makes an application much easier to learn.<P>
<DL>
<DT>A window
<DD><CODE>help</CODE> in its description: the text shown anywhere in the window without help of its
own.<P>
<PRE>
const HELP = 'This window lists the contacts in an address book.|M' +
  'Double-click a contact to change it; drag contacts out to save ' +
  'them as a vCard file; drag vCard or CSV files in to add theirs.';
</PRE>
<DT>An icon
<DD><CODE>help</CODE> in the icon's description. The toolbar passes each button's help on to its icon:<P>
<PRE>
    this.bar = new Toolbar(w, {
      area: this.app.area,
      buttons: [
        { name: 'new', sprite: 'tb_new', action: () =&gt; this.add(),
          help: 'Click SELECT to add a contact.' },
        { name: 'edit', sprite: 'tb_edit', action: () =&gt; this.edit(),
          help: 'Click SELECT to change the chosen contact.' },
        { name: 'delete', sprite: 'tb_delete',
          action: () =&gt; this.remove(),
          help: 'Click SELECT to delete the chosen contacts.' },
        { gap: true },
        { name: 'undo', sprite: 'tb_undo', action: () =&gt; this.undo(),
          help: 'Click SELECT to undo the last change (F8).' },
        { name: 'redo', sprite: 'tb_redo', action: () =&gt; this.redo(),
          help: 'Click SELECT to redo the change (F9).' },
        { gap: true },
        { name: 'save', sprite: 'tb_save',
          action: () =&gt; this.doc.quickSave(),
          help: 'Click SELECT to save the address book (F3).' },
      ],
      search: {
        help: 'Type here to show only the contacts containing it.',
        change: (text) =&gt; { this.search = text; this.refresh(); },
      },
      menu: (ev) =&gt; this.openMenu(ev),
    });
</PRE>
<DT>A menu item
<DD><CODE>help</CODE> in the item: <CODE>{ text: 'Save', ..., help: 'Save the book.' }</CODE>.<P>
<DT>The icon bar icon
<DD><CODE>help</CODE> in <CODE>addIconbarIcon</CODE>.<P>
</DL>
Help text is short, and says what clicking does, in the Style Guide's words: <I>Click SELECT to...</I>.
<CODE>|M</CODE> in help text starts a new line.<P>
The <CODE>Form</CODE> module passes each field's <CODE>help</CODE> on to its icons the same way, so dialogue
boxes can have help too - the Choices box does.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Information</H2>
<I>Info</I> on the icon bar menu shows the desktop's standard box about the program, made with
<CODE>infoBox</CODE> as in the first book. The Style Guide also asks for <I>File &gt; Info</I>, about the document:
its full name, and anything else useful. Contacts shows the file's name, how many contacts it holds, and
whether it has changed:<P>
<PRE>
  /** File &gt; Info: about this address book. */
  infoBox() {
    const d = this.doc, rows = [
      ['File', d.filename || '&lt;untitled&gt;'],
      ['Contacts', String(d.book.size)],
      ['Modified', d.modified ? 'Yes' : 'No'],
    ];
    const icons = rows.flatMap(([label, text], i) =&gt; [
      { x: 8, y: 8 + i * 32, w: 80, h: 28, text: label,
        rjustify: true },
      { x: 96, y: 8 + i * 32, w: 300, h: 28, text, border: true,
        filled: true, bg: 1 },
    ]);
    return this.app.task.createWindow({ title: 'About this file',
      w: 408, h: 112, extent: { w: 408, h: 112 }, icons,
      flags: { title: true } });
  }
</PRE>
The window is made each time the submenu opens, so it is always up to date. <CODE>flatMap</CODE> is like
<CODE>map</CODE>, but each item may give several results - here, two icons for each row - and they are all
put into one array.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>When the desktop shuts down</H2>
When you choose <I>Shutdown</I> from the Task Manager, the desktop sends every task a
<CODE>PreQuit</CODE> message first, asking whether it minds. An application with unsaved work should object,
and ask:<P>
<PRE>
  // the desktop is shutting down: ask about unsaved changes
  task.onMessage('PreQuit', async (msg) =&gt; {
    if (!app.modified) return;
    msg.object?.();                  // &quot;wait&quot;: stop the shutdown
    if (await discardChanges(task,
      'Some address books have changes. Discard them?')) {
      task.quit();
      if (!msg.single) wimp.emit('hotkey:CtrlShiftF12', {});
    }
  });
</PRE>
<CODE>msg.object()</CODE> stops the shutdown. If you then choose to discard the changes, Contacts quits, and
starts the shutdown again (with the key Ctrl-Shift-F12, as RISC OS applications do) - unless the message
was only for Contacts (<CODE>msg.single</CODE>: the Task Manager's <I>Quit</I> on this one task).<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>The !Help file</H2>
The last piece is the <CODE>!Help</CODE> file in the application directory, which the Filer shows when you
choose <I>Help</I> from its menu over <CODE>!Contacts</CODE>. It explains the program for someone who has
just found it:<P>
<PRE>
Contacts - an address book
==========================

Contacts keeps names, addresses, telephone numbers and email
addresses. It is the address book made in the book &quot;Writing Desktop
Applications in JavaScript&quot; (Manuals.JSApps).

Double-click !Contacts, then click SELECT on its icon on the icon bar
to start a new address book. Double-click a Contacts file to open it.

The toolbar
-----------
  New       add a contact (or press Insert)
  Change    change the chosen contact (or double-click it)
  Delete    delete the chosen contacts (or press Delete)
  Undo      undo the last change (F8)
  Redo      redo it (F9)
  Save      save the address book (F3)
  Find      type here to show only the contacts containing the text;
            Escape clears it (F4 puts the caret here)

The list
--------
Click SELECT on a contact to choose it, ADJUST to choose more than
one. The arrow keys, Home, Copy, Page Up and Page Down move through
the list; Return changes the chosen contact; Ctrl-A chooses them all.
F6 changes the order (surname, first name, company).

Other programs
--------------
Drag vCard (.vcf) or CSV files onto the window or the icon to add
their contacts. Drag chosen contacts out of the window to a directory
display to save them as a vCard file. Menu &gt; File &gt; Export saves the
chosen contacts (or all of them) as vCard or CSV.

Choices
-------
Choices... on the icon bar menu sets the order of the list and whether
Contacts asks before deleting. Save keeps them for next time; Set uses
them now only.

Contacts files have the file type &amp;1C4.
</PRE>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>A Style Guide checklist</H2>
Contacts now does what Acorn's RISC OS Style Guide asks of an application that edits documents:<P>
<UL>
<LI>An icon on the icon bar: Select opens a new document; Menu gives Info, Choices... and Quit; files
dropped on it are loaded.<P>
<LI>Documents of its own file type, with an icon, opened by double-clicking - in the copy already running.
<P>
<LI>The file's name in the title, with <CODE>*</CODE> while it has unsaved changes.<P>
<LI>Save with F3 and a Save box; other formats exported with Save boxes of their own.<P>
<LI>Discard/Cancel/Save before closing a changed document, and at shutdown.<P>
<LI>A toolbar of 3D buttons, shaded when they can't be used, repeating what's in the menus.<P>
<LI>Menus built from the current state: items shaded or ticked, shortcut keys shown.<P>
<LI>The standard keys: F3, F4, F6, F8, F9, Ctrl-A, Insert, Delete.<P>
<LI>Dialogue boxes with the default action on the right, Return for it and Escape for Cancel.<P>
<LI>Drag and drop both ways: files in, data from other programs in, contacts out.<P>
<LI>Choices with Default, Cancel, Set and Save, kept in Choices.<P>
<LI>Interactive help everywhere, and a <CODE>!Help</CODE> file.<P>
</UL>
Appendix D has the full checklist, for your own applications.<P>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>What's next</H2>
That is Part One finished: !Contacts is a real application, in about 1,500 lines of your own code, and a
toolkit of six modules you can use in other programs. Part Two builds a much bigger one - an organiser in
the spirit of Lotus Organizer, with a diary, a to-do list, a notepad, a planner and anniversaries in a
ring binder - and its address section will be !Contacts' modules, used unchanged.<P>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>Undo: describe each change as a command with <CODE>do</CODE> and <CODE>undo</CODE>; an
<CODE>Undo</CODE> object carries them out and keeps stacks of done and undone commands. Give each document
its own.<P>
<LI>A menu item's text can be a function, worked out each time the menu opens.<P>
<LI>Choices: a Choices... box with Default, Cancel, Set and Save, saved with <CODE>choices.write</CODE>
in the Choices directory and read with <CODE>choices.read</CODE>.<P>
<LI>Interactive help: <CODE>help</CODE> on windows, icons, menu items and the icon bar icon; <CODE>|M</CODE>
for a new line.<P>
<LI>File &gt; Info shows the document's details; <CODE>PreQuit</CODE> lets you protect unsaved work at
shutdown.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Make the number of changes kept for undo a choice, from 10 to 200.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Add a choice for whether the email column is shown.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Give the list's column headings interactive help: <I>Click SELECT to sort by this column</I> -
and make clicking a heading sort by it. (The list's <CODE>ListView</CODE> draws the headings in the top
<CODE>20</CODE> pixels below the toolbar.)<P>
<DT><DD>4  Undo remembers changes after saving. Some programs clear it when the document is saved; others
mark the point, so undoing past it puts the <CODE>*</CODE> back. Which do you prefer? Make Contacts put the
<CODE>*</CODE> back correctly.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Add <CODE>undoLimit: 50</CODE> to <CODE>DEFAULTS</CODE>, a field for it in the Choices box (with
<CODE>allowed: '0-9'</CODE>), and in <CODE>Document</CODE>: <CODE>this.undo = new
Undo(app.prefs.undoLimit)</CODE>. Check the number in the form's <CODE>check</CODE> function:
<PRE>
check: (v) =&gt; (+v.undoLimit &lt; 10 || +v.undoLimit &gt; 200
  ? { field: 'undoLimit', message: 'From 10 to 200, please' }
  : null),
</PRE>
Two more things are needed. <CODE>openChoices</CODE> copies only <CODE>sort</CODE> and
<CODE>confirm</CODE> from the box into <CODE>prefs</CODE>, so it must copy the new one too; and a writable
field's value is a string, so turn it into a number as it's copied:
<PRE>
Object.assign(prefs, { sort, confirm: r.values.confirm,
  undoLimit: +r.values.undoLimit });
</PRE>
(A document already open keeps the limit it was made with.)<P>
<DT><DD>2  Add <CODE>email: true</CODE> to <CODE>DEFAULTS</CODE> and an option field, and build the
columns from the choices, then call <CODE>setItems</CODE> again when they change (or make a new
<CODE>ListView</CODE>).<P>
<DT><DD>3  The list takes every click on its window - including a click on the headings, which it
ignores - and a handler that returns <CODE>true</CODE> stops the ones added after it. So add a
<CODE>'click'</CODE> handler to the window in <CODE>MainWindow</CODE>'s constructor <I>before</I> the
<CODE>ListView</CODE> is made. The headings stay at the top of the visible area as the list scrolls, so a
click is on them when <CODE>ev.y</CODE> is between <CODE>w.scrollY + this.bar.height</CODE> and
20 more. Add up the columns' widths to find which one holds <CODE>ev.x</CODE>; only Name has an order to
sort by (<CODE>setSort('last')</CODE>), unless you add more to <CODE>SORTS</CODE>. Return
<CODE>true</CODE> when the click was on the headings, and nothing otherwise so the list gets it. The help goes
in a <CODE>'helprequest'</CODE> handler on the window, setting <CODE>ev.text</CODE> when the pointer is over
the headings, tested the same way.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Remember which command was last done when the document was saved, and compare after each undo
or redo:
<PRE>
// in save:
this.savedAt = this.undo.done.at(-1);
// in the Document constructor:
this.undo.on('change', () =&gt;
  this.setModified(this.undo.done.at(-1) !== this.savedAt));
</PRE>
(The book's <CODE>'change'</CODE> handler still sets modified when a change is made; this corrects it after
undo and redo.)<P>
</DL>

